 "key_big": "10 lakh = 1 million; 1 crore = 10 million",
 "key_sub": "Indian commas: 3 digits, then groups of 2 (1,23,45,678).",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p><b>Indian system</b>: thousand (1,000), lakh (1,00,000), crore (1,00,00,000). Commas: the first after 3 digits from the right, then after every 2.</p><p><b>International system</b>: thousand, million (1,000,000), billion (1,000,000,000). Commas after every 3 digits.</p><p>10 lakh = 1 million, 1 crore = 10 million, 100 crore = 1 billion.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Read 4,05,20,300 in the Indian system.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>4 crore, 5 lakh, 20 thousand, 300</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 4 crore 5 lakh 20 thousand 300</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>How many million is 35 lakh?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>10 lakh = 1 million</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 3.5 million</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Thinking 1 lakh = 1 million (it is 100 thousand).</li><li>Putting Indian commas every 3 digits.</li><li>Reading the place value from the left instead of the right.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How many lakh are in a crore?",
   "a": "100 lakh = 1 crore."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do lakh and million compare?",
   "a": "10 lakh = 1 million, so 1 lakh is one-tenth of a million."
  },
  {
   "q": "How are commas placed in the Indian system?",
   "a": "The first comma comes after the hundreds place, then after every two digits: 12,34,56,789."
  }
